# PR review

The fix direction is sound: capturing the baseline once per control source (including `null`) fixes `data-dirty` never clearing for null-valued controls, and the new tests pin it well. One confirmed behavior change worth an explicit decision before merge.

## Bugs (1)

### 1. 🟠 Remounting the same control resets the dirty baseline to the current value

**Location:** `packages/react/src/internals/field-register-control/useFieldControlRegistration.ts:86`

`useRegisterFieldControl` creates a fresh `Symbol()` per component instance, so an unmount/remount of the *same* control is indistinguishable from a control swap: the new symbol passes the gate and `initialValue` is recaptured as whatever the value is at remount time. Before this PR, a non-null baseline was never overwritten, so it survived remounts.

**Failure scenario:** Controlled Select inside a persistent `Field.Root`, initial value `'a'`. User picks `'b'` (dirty), the control is conditionally hidden and reshown (e.g. collapsible section) while `Field.Root` stays mounted, then the user picks `'a'` again. The field now stays `data-dirty` (`'a' !== 'b'`) and would read as clean at `'b'`. Verified locally: this exact test passes on `master` and fails with the PR applied. (StrictMode is fine — the symbol persists across effect re-runs; only a real remount triggers it.)

**Fix:** If recapture-on-swap is the intended semantic this may be acceptable, but it should be pinned with a test. A heuristic that fixes the remount case while keeping the swap tests green — skip recapture when the field is already dirty:

```diff
   function syncInitialValue() {
     const source = activeFieldControlSourceRef.current;
     if (syncedInitialValueSourceRef.current === source) {
       return;
     }

     syncedInitialValueSourceRef.current = source;

+    // A new control source normally recaptures the baseline (control swap), but if the
+    // user has already dirtied the field, treat the remounting control as the same
+    // logical field and keep the original baseline.
+    if (markedDirtyRef.current) {
+      return;
+    }
+
     const registration = registrationRef.current;
     if (!registration) {
       return;
     }
```

The trade-off: a swap *after* interaction now keeps the old baseline instead of recapturing — ambiguous either way; this picks "preserve".

## Docs (1)

### 2. ℹ️ New per-source gate deserves a one-line comment

`syncInitialValue` now encodes a non-obvious contract: capture the baseline exactly once per control source (even `null`), recapture only when a different control registers. A short comment would stop a future "simplification" from reintroducing the original bug (covered by the suggestion above if taken).

## Verdict

**Approve after nits** — the confirmed remount baseline reset is narrow and possibly acceptable, but should be an explicit decision with a pinning test.

